message.reasoning_content:
Key bugs: parse_u64 sets out=0 first, violating "out unchanged on failure" (e.g. "12x" leaves out=0 instead of caller's value; also overflow wraps silently, e.g. "18446744073789591617" returns wrapped value instead of rejecting). Also empty string accepted (returns true, out=0) — contract says nonempty only. Also parse_u64 marked noexcept but fine.

Registry: get returns string_view into vector while holding lock only during call; but vector push_back may reallocate/move strings, invalidating returned views — readers retain views after get returns while add keeps calling → dangling string_view. Also get(i) no bounds check (out of range UB). Also mutex held during get but view escapes lock — that's fine for mutex but the reallocation is the defect. Also names[i] with i>=size UB.

Also parse_u64 overflow: contract says reject above UINT64_MAX; code wraps silently. And failure leaves out unchanged violated by out=0 at top. Also empty accepted violates nonempty.

Minimal examples:
1. parse_u64("12x", out) with out=7 → returns true? No: loop: '1' ok, '2' ok, 'x' → return false but out already modified to 12 (and set to 0 initially). Contract violation: out changed on failure.
2. parse_u64("") → returns true, out=0; contract requires nonempty → should fail.
3. parse_u64("18446744073789591617") (UINT64_MAX+2) wraps → returns true with wrong value; should reject.
4. Registry: reader holds sv from get(0); writer add("x") causes vector reallocation → strings move → sv dangles. Use-after-free.
5. get(names.size()) → out-of-bounds UB (no bounds check).
6. Also noexcept parse: fine. Also mutex: get returns view while lock released — but contract says readers retain after get returns; the mutex doesn't protect the string lifetime; reallocation is the defect. Also add while reader holds view: push_back move of string invalidates view even without reallocation? Moving a string to new storage invalidates views into old storage. Yes.

Fixes: parse_u64: check empty, don't touch out until success (use local accumulator), detect overflow (check out > (UINT64_MAX - d)/10 before multiply, or compare against max/10). Registry: store names in a way that never invalidates views — e.g. deque of shared_ptr<string>, or vector of unique_ptr with stable addresses? vector of shared_ptr<const string> gives stable pointers; or std::deque<std::string> with push_back at back only — deque never reallocates? Deque blocks: iterators/refs remain valid across push_back at either end (C++17 guarantees refs not invalidated by push_back/pemplace_back). But get returns string_view into deque element; push_back at back doesn't invalidate refs to existing elements — guaranteed. But pop_front not used. So deque works. Simpler: vector<std::shared_ptr<const std::string>> — element addresses stable, views into pointed-to strings stable. Bounds check in get.

Corrected parse_u64 in one cpp block:

```cpp
bool parse_u64(std::string_view s, std::uint64_t& out) noexcept {
    if (s.empty()) return false;
    std::uint64_t acc = 0;
    for (char c : s) {
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') return false;
        std::uint64_t d = c - '0';
        if (acc > (UINT64_MAX - d) / 10) return false;  // careful: (max-d)/10
        acc = acc * 10 + d;
    }
    out = acc;
    return true;
}
```
Overflow check: acc*10+d > max iff acc > (max-d)/10. Correct.

Registry fix: keep names in std::deque<std::string> (push_back only; C++17 guarantees no reference invalidation), bounds-check get. Or shared_ptr.

Word budget ~650. Six findings max. Write concisely.
